'''Liish Kozlowski''' ({{IPAc-en|l|i|ʃ|_||k|ə|z|ˈ|l|aʊ|s|k|i}};<ref>{{cite AV media |date=October 29, 2022|title=Minnesota House District 8B Forum
|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eHI15okftUI|publisher=PBS North|via=[[YouTube]]|access-date=August 23, 2024 |time= 00:11}}</ref> {{langx|oj|Ozaawaa Anakwad}}; born January 12, 1988) is an American politician serving as a member of the [[Minnesota House of Representatives]] since 2023. A member of the [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party|Minnesota Democratic-Farmer-Labor Party]] (DFL), Kozlowski represents District 8B in northeastern [[Minnesota]], which includes the city of [[Duluth, Minnesota|Duluth]] and parts of [[St. Louis County, Minnesota|St. Louis County]]. They are the first openly trans-nonbinary state legislator in Minnesota.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Kozlowski, Liish - Legislator Record - Minnesota Legislators Past & Present |url=https://www.lrl.mn.gov/legdb/fulldetail?ID=15595 |access-date=2025-11-24 |website=www.lrl.mn.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Rep. Liish Kozlowski (08B) - Minnesota House of Representatives |url=https://www.house.mn.gov/members/profile/15595 |access-date=2025-11-24 |website=www.house.mn.gov}}</ref>

== Early life, education and career ==
Born in [[Duluth, Minnesota]], Kozlowski is the child of a Mexican union electrician father and a [[Fond du Lac Band of Lake Superior Chippewa|Grand Portage/Fond du Lac Band]] Ojibwe mother.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last1=Hollingsworth |first1=Jana |last2=Lawler |first2=Christa |date=August 5, 2022 |title=Voter's guide for Duluth and St. Louis County primary races |url=https://www.startribune.com/voters-guide-for-duluth-and-st-louis-county-primary-races/600195939/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune}}</ref> They were raised by their grandmother and adopted in adulthood by a native family.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Ki |first=Nicole |date=2023-06-21 |title=ChangeMakers: For two-spirit legislator Alicia Kozlowski, community is everything |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2023/06/21/changemakers-for-two-spirit-legislator-alicia-kozlowski-community-is-everything |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=MPR News |language=en}}</ref> Their adoptive father, Ray "Skip" Sandman, was an Anishinaabe spiritual leader and ran twice for [[Minnesota's 8th congressional district|Minnesota's 8th Congressional District]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hollingsworth |first=Jana |date=November 3, 2022 |title=Skip Sandman, an Anishinaabe spiritual healer, dies at 68 |url=https://www.startribune.com/skip-sandman-an-anishinaabe-spiritual-healer-dies-at-68/600221726/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune}}</ref><ref name=":2" />

Kozlowski is a first-generation college graduate, earning a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in history from the [[University of Minnesota Duluth]] in 2009 and a [[Master of Business Administration]] from the [[College of St. Scholastica]] in 2017.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-11-09 |title=Minnesota's first trans nonbinary elected state representative wins in Duluth |url=https://www.bemidjipioneer.com/news/minnesota/minnesotas-first-nonbinary-elected-state-representative-wins-in-duluth |access-date=2023-01-08 |website=Bemidji Pioneer |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1" />

From 2019 to 2021, Kozlowski worked as a community relations officer for the City of Duluth under Mayor [[Emily Larson]], where they helped create a Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women, Girls, and Two Spirit People Reward Fund, the first in Minnesota.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Galioto |first=Katie |date=June 19, 2020 |title=Duluth pushes to remove 'chief' from job titles, calling it offensive to Indigenous people |url=https://www.startribune.com/duluth-pushes-to-remove-chief-from-job-titles-calling-it-offensive-to-indigenous-people/571342532/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lawler |first=Christa |date=January 25, 2022 |title=Duluth creates reward fund to help solve crimes against Indigenous women |url=https://www.startribune.com/duluth-creates-reward-fund-to-help-solve-crimes-against-indigenous-women/600139547/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune}}</ref>

== Minnesota House of Representatives ==
Kozlowski was elected to the [[Minnesota House of Representatives]] in November 2022. They first ran after four-term [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party|DFL]] incumbent [[Jennifer Schultz]] announced they would not seek reelection and would run for the [[Minnesota's 8th congressional district|8th Congressional District]].<ref name=":0" /> Kozlowski defeated Duluth City Council president Arik Forsman in the DFL primary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lawler |first=Christa |title=First-timer Alicia Kozlowski will face Becky Hall in House District 8B race |url=https://www.startribune.com/first-timer-kozlowski-will-face-hall-in-house-district-8b-race/600197231/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune|date=10 August 2022 }}</ref> When they took office, Kozlowski became the first openly trans non-binary member of the [[Minnesota Legislature]] and the second [[two-spirit]] member after [[Susan Allen (politician)|Susan Allen]], in addition to the first Mexican, Ojibwe, and trans non-binary or two-spirit person to represent Duluth.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Asher |first=Abe |date=2022-11-23 |title=Alicia Kozlowski elected Minnesota's first trans non-binary legislator |url=http://sahanjournal.com/democracy-politics/alicia-kozlowski-first-nonbinary-legislator-duluth-minnesota-lgbtq/ |access-date=2023-01-08 |website=Sahan Journal |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lawler |first=Christa |date=November 9, 2022 |title=Zeleznikar poised to oust longtime Rep. Mary Murphy in Minnesota House District 3B |url=https://www.startribune.com/zeleznikar-poised-to-oust-longtime-rep-mary-murphy-in-minnesota-house-district-3b/600223832/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune}}</ref>

Kozlowski serves on the Capital Investment and Economic Development Finance and Policy Committees and is co-vice chair of the Housing Finance and Policy and Labor and Industry Finance and Policy Committees.<ref name=":0" /> They co-chair the House People of Color and Indigenous (POCI) Caucus and are a member of the legislature's first Queer Caucus, made up of LGBTQ+ members.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stroozas |first=Sam |date= 2023-01-17 |title='Shaking things up': Minnesota's LGBTQ lawmakers see new strength at Capitol |url= https://www.mprnews.org/story/2023/01/17/were-shaking-things-up-minnesotas-lgbtq-lawmakers-see-new-strength-at-capitol |access-date= 2023-08-10 |website= MPR News |language= en}}</ref>

Kozlowski was highlighted as part of [[Minnesota Public Radio]]'s 2023 "Changemakers" series, which showcases Minnesotans from diverse backgrounds making an impact in the state.<ref name=":2" />

=== Political positions ===
Kozlowski campaigned on increasing support for working families through universal childcare and fully-funded education, protecting the environment through "prove it first" mining permit legislation, and affordable healthcare for all.<ref name=":1" />

Kozlowski is [[Abortion-rights movements|pro-choice]] and supports increasing abortion access for people of color, gender-expansive people, and immigrants.<ref>{{Cite web |last=The Associated Press |date=2022-12-27 |title=What Minnesota's most diverse Legislature means for policy |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2022/12/27/what-minnesotas-most-diverse-legislature-means-for-policy |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=MPR News |language=en}}</ref> They have spoken out in favor of legislation making Minnesota a "trans refuge state" for those seeking [[gender-affirming care]] and have pushed for creating inclusive schools and increasing housing access for disadvantaged communities.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Moini |first=Nina |date=2023-04-21 |title=This family moved to Minnesota to access gender-affirming care. More might soon |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2023/04/21/this-family-moved-to-minnesota-to-access-genderaffirming-care-more-might-soon |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=MPR News |language=en}}</ref> Kozlowski authored a law adding [[gender-neutral bathrooms]] in Minnesota schools in 2023.<ref name=":2" /> They also authored a bill to ban [[Intersex medical interventions|nonessential cosmetic procedures]] on [[intersex]] children under 12, which did not make it to a vote.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Forgrave |first1=Reid |title=Yes, intersex people exist. A Twin Cities advocate is fighting for their rights. |url=https://www.startribune.com/intersex-advocate-wants-something-small-for-minnesotans-to-know-intersex-people-exist/600375200 |access-date=24 November 2025 |work=[[Minnesota Star Tribune]] |date=June 21, 2024}}</ref>

Kozlowski championed efforts to increase funding for the state's [[Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women|Missing and Murdered Indigenous Relatives]] Office. Native American women, girls, and Two Spirit relatives are only 1% of Minnesota's population but made up 8% of all murdered women, girls, and Two Spirit people from 2010 through 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bierschbach |first=Briana |title=Leaders push for more funding to find missing and murdered Indigenous Minnesotans |url=https://www.startribune.com/leaders-push-for-more-funding-to-find-missing-and-murdered-indigenous-minnesotans/600251630/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Star Tribune|date=15 February 2023 }}</ref>

