'''Brenton Patrick Chokei Kawika Awa''' (born February 19, 1986)<ref name="Freshman">{{cite news |last1=Nakaso |first1=Dan |title=Freshman lawmakers aim to rebuild Hawaii's GOP |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2023/01/25/hawaii-news/freshman-lawmakers-aim-to-rebuild-hawaiis-gop/ |access-date=November 7, 2023 |work=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |date=January 25, 2023}}</ref> is an American politician and former television news anchor has served as the Minority Leader of the [[Hawaii Senate]] since 2024. A member of the [[Hawaii Republican Party|Republican party]], he has represented the 23rd district in the [[Hawaii Senate]] since 2022, which includes the communities of [[Kāneʻohe, Hawaii|Kāne‘ohe]], [[Kahaluʻu, Hawaii|Kahalu‘u]] thru [[Lāʻie, Hawaii|Lā‘ie]], [[Kahuku, Hawaii|Kahuku]] to [[Mokulēʻia, Hawaii|Mokulē‘ia]], [[Schofield Barracks]], and [[Kunia Camp, Hawaii|Kunia Camp]] on the island of [[Oʻahu|O'ahu]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Legislative Members |url=https://capitolwebsite.azurewebsites.net/legislature/memberpage.aspx?member=225&year=2025 |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=capitolwebsite.azurewebsites.net}}</ref>

Awa is the Republican nominee for [[Hawaii's 2nd congressional district]] in the [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Hawaii#District 2|2026 U.S. House elections]], challenging Democratic incumbent nominee [[Jill Tokuda]].

== Early life and education ==
Brenton Patrick Chokei Kawika Awa was born on February 19, 1986 in [[Kaʻaʻawa, Hawaii|Kaʻaʻawa]] on the island of [[Oʻahu|O'ahu]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Senator Brenton Awa (R); Minority Leader |url=https://www.capitol.hawaii.gov/legislature/memberpage.aspx?member=225&year=2025|access-date=August 10, 2026|website=Hawaiʻi State Legislature}}</ref> Awa was raised in [[Kahaluʻu, Hawaii|Kahalu'u]] and would graduate from [[Kahuku High & Intermediate School]] in 2004.<ref name="Brenton Awa">{{Cite web |title=Brenton Awa |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Brenton_Awa |access-date=January 21, 2023 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref> Awa would graduate from [[PennWest Clarion|Pennsylvania Western University – Clarion]] (formerly Clarion University of Pennsylvania) while earning a degree in [[Speech|speech communication]] and leadership.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2006-2007 Clarion University |url=https://armdrag.com/matburn?team=398&year=2007 |access-date=2025-02-26 |website=Armdrag|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Awa would wrestle during his time at Kahuku and PennWest Clarion.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ArchivesSpace Public Interface {{!}} Clarion University Archives {{!}} 2005-2006 Clarion Wrestling Media Guide |url=https://as-clarion.klnpa.org/repositories/2/archival_objects/5481 |access-date=2025-02-26 |website=as-clarion.klnpa.org}}</ref><ref name=":0" /> In 2008, Awa briefly coached Kahuku's junior varsity wrestling team, which won the [[Oahu Interscholastic Association]] championship.<ref name=":2" />

== Career ==

=== Media career===
In January 2009, Awa began his career in television news for [[KGMB|KGMB9 News]] in [[Honolulu]]. Awa would begin as an intern, transitioning into roles of an [[associate producer]] and [[Video journalism|video journalist]].<ref name=":2" /> In 2015, [[KITV]] hired Awa as a reporter, where he would be promoted to morning anchor and eventually evening anchor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blair |first=Chad |title=Brenton Awa Running For Hawaii State Senate |url=https://www.civilbeat.org/beat/brenton-awa-running-for-hawaii-state-senate/ |access-date=January 21, 2023 |website=Honolulu Civil Beat |date=October 11, 2021 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=ON THE MOVE – DECEMBER 2018 {{!}} NATAS SF/NorCal |url=https://emmysf.tv/membership-2/december-2018-off-camera-2-2-2/on-the-move-december-2018/ |access-date=January 21, 2023 |website=emmysf.tv}}</ref> 

Awa was fired from KITV in July 2021 after punching a door in the newsroom, which he claims was due to an order that went against the [[Journalism ethics and standards|fundamental ethics of journalism]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Nakaso |first=Dan |date=2023-01-25 |title=Freshman lawmakers aim to rebuild Hawaii's GOP |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2023/01/25/hawaii-news/freshman-lawmakers-aim-to-rebuild-hawaiis-gop/ |access-date=2025-03-21 |website=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |language=en}}</ref> Awa sued the station over his firing.<ref name=":0" /> He then worked as a substitute teacher at [[Kahuku High & Intermediate School|Kahuku High School]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lovell |first=Blaze |date=2022-09-27 |title=Former TV Anchor And Longtime Activist Face Off In Rural Oahu Senate Race |url=https://www.civilbeat.org/2022/09/former-tv-anchor-and-longtime-activist-face-off-in-rural-oahu-senate-race/ |access-date=2025-03-21 |website=Honolulu Civil Beat |language=en}}</ref>

=== Political career ===
Awa was first elected to the [[Hawaii Senate]] in [[2022 Hawaii Senate election|November 2022]], defeating incumbent Democrat [[Gil Riviere]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dowd |first=Eddie |date=November 10, 2022 |title=Former TV anchor defeats incumbent in state Senate race |url=https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2022/11/10/former-tv-anchor-brenton-awa-defeats-8-year-incumbent-state-senate-race/ |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=Hawaii News Now |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=State of Hawaii |date=November 8, 2022 |title=GENERAL ELECTION 2022 - State of Hawaii - Statewide |url=https://elections.hawaii.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022-general-report-4-histatewide.pdf |url-status=live |website=State of Hawaii Office of Elections}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Blair |first=Chad |date=January 1, 2023 |title=A Conversation With Hawaii's Newest GOP State Senator |url=https://www.civilbeat.org/2023/01/a-conversation-with-hawaiis-newest-gop-state-senator/ |access-date=January 21, 2023 |website=Honolulu Civil Beat |language=en}}</ref> Leading up to the 2024 election, Awa pledged not to campaign or accept donations.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 22, 2024 |title=These 2 Senate races could flip seats in the state Legislature |url=https://www.hawaiipublicradio.org/local-news/2024-10-22/key-senate-races-election-flip-seats-state-legislature |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=Hawai'i Public Radio |language=en}}</ref> Awa was narrowly re-elected in [[2024 Hawaii Senate election|November 2024]], becoming the Senate minority leader.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 7, 2024 |title=Reelected state Sen. Brenton Awa slated to become minority leader |url=https://www.hawaiipublicradio.org/local-news/2024-11-06/senate-brenton-awa-minority-leader |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=Hawai'i Public Radio |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Blog |first=The Sunshine |date=January 19, 2025 |title=The Sunshine Blog: What Has Gotten Into Sen. Brenton Awa? |url=https://www.civilbeat.org/2025/01/the-sunshine-blog-what-has-gotten-into-sen-brenton-awa/ |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=Honolulu Civil Beat |language=en}}</ref>

During his first term, Awa vocalized support by introducing a bill to bar non-[[Citizenship of the United States|United States citizens]] from purchasing land in Hawaii to allow local residents to deal with the state's [[Housing crisis in the United States|housing crisis]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Martinez |first=Jolanie |date=February 7, 2024 |title=Crowds gather to testify on bill that would ban foreigners from buying property in Hawaii |url=https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2024/02/07/foreigners-would-be-banned-buying-property-hawaii-under-new-proposed-bill-gaining-support-local-homebuyers/ |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=Hawaii News Now |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=February 6, 2024 |title=Should foreigners be banned from purchasing land in Hawaii? |url=https://www.khon2.com/local-news/should-foreigners-be-banned-from-purchasing-land-in-hawaii/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240925133703/https://www.khon2.com/local-news/should-foreigners-be-banned-from-purchasing-land-in-hawaii/ |archive-date=September 25, 2024 |access-date=February 25, 2025 |work=KHON2 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=New Hawaii bill would ban foreigners from buying land on the Islands |url=https://abcnews.go.com/US/new-hawaii-bill-ban-foreigners-buying-land-islands/story?id=107021311 |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> Concerns were raised about the legality of the bill and whether it may violate the Constitution.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Farrell |first=James |title=Here's Why Some Hawaii Lawmakers Want To Ban Foreigners From Purchasing Land |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamesfarrell/2024/02/08/heres-why-some-hawaii-lawmakers-want-to-ban-foreigners-from-purchasing-land/ |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Consillio |first=Kristen |date=February 6, 2024 |title=Legislative bills aim to ban foreigners from buying property in Hawaii |url=https://www.kitv.com/news/local/legislative-bills-aim-to-ban-foreigners-from-buying-property-in-hawaii/article_bb119fa8-c56c-11ee-bb48-6f1c1304fb5e.html |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=KITV Island News |language=en}}</ref> 

In 2023, Awa worked with the state and the [[United States Army]] to fix a longstanding water leak on the [[North Shore (Oʻahu)|North Shore]] of Oahu.<ref>{{Cite news |date=October 2, 2023 |title=Decade long water leak being fixed after millions of gallons wasted |url=https://www.khon2.com/local-news/decade-long-water-leak-being-fixed-after-millions-of-gallons-wasted/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231205181347/https://www.khon2.com/local-news/decade-long-water-leak-being-fixed-after-millions-of-gallons-wasted/ |archive-date=December 5, 2023 |access-date=February 25, 2025 |work=KHON2 |language=en-US}}</ref> He would also establish a side-of-the-road fruit tree project in his hometown of [[Kahaluʻu, Hawaii|Kahalu'u]] by planting [[Breadfruit|''ʻulu'']] and [[Syzygium malaccense|mountain apple]] trees for public use and consumption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Naktin |first=Eric |date=December 16, 2023 |title=Kahalu'u community members unite and plant a foundation for future food sources |url=https://www.kitv.com/news/kahaluu-community-members-unite-and-plant-a-foundation-for-future-food-sources/article_68c7d588-9c99-11ee-8929-4f221d2374af.html |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=KITV Island News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Martinez |first=Jolanie |date=December 17, 2023 |title=Small project to plant fruit trees along highway seen as big step toward sustainability |url=https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2023/12/17/small-project-plant-fruit-trees-along-highway-seen-big-step-toward-sustainability/ |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=Hawaii News Now |language=en}}</ref> He would establish a similar project in [[Haleʻiwa, Hawaii|Haleʻiwa]] to restore coconut tree populations and eradicate invasive [[Oryctes rhinoceros|coconut rhinoceros beetles]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Napuunoa |first=Nicole |date=September 7, 2024 |title=Project aims to fight invasive beetle |url=https://www.khon2.com/local-news/project-aims-to-fight-invasive-beetle/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 11, 2026 |work=KHON-TV}}</ref> 

Awa would also be contacted to organize a public homecoming concert for former Kahuku resident [[Iam Tongi]], who won [[American Idol season 21|American Idol's 21st season]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=May 15, 2023 |title=Iam Tongi makes it to Top 3 of 'American Idol' |url=https://www.khon2.com/local-news/iam-tongi-makes-it-to-top-3-of-american-idol/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929160407/https://www.khon2.com/local-news/iam-tongi-makes-it-to-top-3-of-american-idol/ |archive-date=September 29, 2023 |access-date=February 25, 2025 |work=KHON2 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Iam Tongi Concert Preps |url=https://www.khon2.com/local-news/homecoming-preps-underway-as-iam-tongi-moves-to-top-5-on-idol/}}</ref> During his first term as a senator, Awa cast more than 1,000 ''[[Yeas and Nays|nay]]'' votes,{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}} being the sole ''nay'' on 10 percent of all bills passed during that time.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Gomes |first=Andrew |date=June 10, 2024 |title=First-term state lawmaker Sen. Brenton Awa stands out as unconventional |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2024/06/10/hawaii-news/senator-rising-in-lone-dissent/ |access-date=February 25, 2025 |website=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |language=en}}</ref>

On March 8, 2023, an incident occurred at a virtual [[Kahaluʻu, Hawaii|Kahalu'u]] Neighborhood Board meeting on banning commercial aquarium fishing where Awa said that Native Hawaiian cultural leader, Leialoha Rocky Kaluhiwa, "would have been hung” for criticizing his vote against the ban.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Huff |first=Daryl |date=2023-05-09 |title=Lawmaker won’t apologize after saying Hawaiian leader ‘would have been hung’ for criticizing him |url=https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2023/05/09/complaint-filed-against-state-lawmaker-who-told-critic-public-meeting-she-would-be-hung/ |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Hawaii News Now |language=en}}</ref> Awa has said he has no intention to apologize following criticism.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yurow |first=Jeremy |date=2026-04-02 |title=Hawaii bill to ban commercial aquarium fishing stalls |url=https://www.courthousenews.com/hawaii-bill-to-ban-commercial-reef-fishing-stalls |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Courthouse News Service |language=en-us}}</ref>

The Hawaiʻi State Ethics Commission would allege in late 2024 that Awa violated the State Ethics Code's Fair Treatment Law following a video posted on his official Instagram account.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blog |first=The Sunshine |date=2025-11-09 |title=The Sunshine Blog: Charter Commission Slammed With More Than 200 Proposals |url=https://civilbeat.org/2025/11/the-sunshine-blog-charter-commission-gets-slammed-with-more-than-200-proposals/ |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Honolulu Civil Beat |language=en}}</ref> The Commission claimed that Awa used taxpayer-funded facilities, platforms, and office time to show support for [[Samantha DeCorte]], his then-office manager, following public complaints.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mizuo |first=Ashley |date=2025-11-04 |title=State Sen. Awa contests election ethics violation over social media video |url=https://www.hawaiipublicradio.org/local-news/2025-11-04/state-sen-awa-contests-election-ethics-violation-social-media |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Hawai'i Public Radio |language=en}}</ref> They would cite the ending of the video as promotion for DeCorte: <blockquote>Getting Sam in as [[Waiʻanae, Hawaii|Waianae]] Senator would unlock our full potential in both communities to represent not just there, but represent the entire State of Hawaii. You guys know what to do. Aloha.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=HNN |date=2025-12-07 |title=State senator decries fine for video violating campaign law |url=https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2025/12/07/state-senator-decries-fine-video-violating-campaign-law/ |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Hawaii News Now |language=en}}</ref></blockquote>Awa claimed that the video was a farewell for DeCorte that highlighted her contributions to his office's work and her reasons for departing.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mizuo |first=Ashley |date=2025-11-05 |title=State Sen. Awa contests election ethics violation over social media video |url=https://www.hawaiipublicradio.org/local-news/2025-11-04/state-sen-awa-contests-election-ethics-violation-social-media |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Hawai'i Public Radio |language=en}}</ref> He was fined in late 2025 following a final ruling by the Commission.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gomes |first=Andrew |date=2025-12-05 |title=Hawaii Ethics Commission fines state Sen. Brenton Awa |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2025/12/05/hawaii-news/ethics-panel-fines-state-senator-brenton-awa/ |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |language=en}}</ref>

Awa gained [[Viral video|media popularity]] following his response to a video of a tourist being beaten after throwing a rock at an endangered [[Hawaiian monk seal]] on [[Maui]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-05-08 |title=Tourist Mauls Beloved Maui Monk Seal With Rock, Then Gets Beaten Up On Camera (Video) |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/tourist-mauls-beloved-maui-monk-192906912.html |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-US}}</ref> In a speech during a committee hearing, Awa would present a letter of recognition to the man who beat the tourist, whom he kept anonymous.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-05-11 |title=‘Ambassador of Aloha’ Beats Up Monk Seal Attacker on Maui as Officials Cheer (Video) |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/ambassador-aloha-beats-monk-seal-183537182.html |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-US}}</ref> Awa would nickname him the "Ambassador of [[Aloha]]," and suggested having the video be shown on flights to the state.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shafiq |first=Jeanine Santucci and Saman |title=Man arrested, accused of hurling rock at endangered monk seal's head |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/crime/2026/05/14/monk-seal-endangered-rock-throw-arrest/90074594007/ |access-date=2026-08-10 |website=USA TODAY |language=en-US}}</ref>

Awa would become the Republican nominee for [[Hawaii's 2nd congressional district]] in the [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Hawaii#District 2|2026 U.S. House elections]], running unopposed in the Republican primary election.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-08-08 |title=Hawaii Second Congressional District Primary 2026: Live Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/08/08/us/elections/results-hawaii-us-house-2-primary.html |access-date=2026-08-10 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

