'''Mark S. Conway Jr.''' (born 1989) is an American nonprofit executive and politician serving as a member of the [[Baltimore City Council]] from the 4th district since December 2020. A [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]], Conway was first elected to the role in 2020 then re-elected unopposed in [[2024 Baltimore City Council election|2024]].

Prior to running for elected office, Conway was an executive vice president at the [[Chesapeake Conservancy]], executive director of the Baltimore Tree Trust, and deputy director of [[CitiStat]] within the Office of the [[Mayor of Baltimore]]. He ran for the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. House of Representatives]] in [[Maryland's 7th congressional district]] election in [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Maryland#District 7|2026]], losing to incumbent [[Kweisi Mfume]] in the primary election with 24.5% of the vote.

==Early life and education==
Conway was born in 1989 in [[The Bronx]], [[New York City]], and raised there by parents who worked in [[law enforcement]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://marylandmatters.org/2025/10/03/baltimore-councilmember-conway-takes-first-steps-toward-mfume-challenge/|title=Baltimore Councilmember Conway takes first steps toward Mfume challenge|first=Josh|last=Kurtz|date=October 3, 2026|website=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref><ref name="MSA page">{{cite web|url=https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/mdmanual/36loc/bcity/leg/html/msa18279.html|title=MARK CONWAY, Member, City Council (Democrat)|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=Maryland Manual On-Line|publisher=[[Maryland State Archives]]}}</ref> He graduated from the [[University of Maryland, College Park]] with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in government, politics and philosophy in 2010 as well as a [[Master of Public Policy]] in 2013.<ref name="SPP Speaker">{{cite web|url=https://spp.umd.edu/news/meet-baltimore-city-councilmember-mark-conway-mpp-13|title=Meet Baltimore City Councilmember Mark Conway MPP ’13|date=December 16, 2021|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[University of Maryland School of Public Policy]]}}</ref> While an undergraduate, Conway participated in the College Park Scholars Public Leadership Program and the [[Pete Rawlings|Rawlings]] Undergraduate Leadership Fellows Program.<ref name="Alumnus"/>

==Career==
While at graduate school, Conway worked part-time at the [[U.S. Environmental Protection Agency]]. Upon graduating, he worked as a [[CitiStat]] analyst in the Office of the [[Mayor of Baltimore]] from 2012 to 2014 before being promoted deputy director of the office.<ref name="SPP Speaker"/><ref name="Alumnus">{{cite web|url=https://spp.umd.edu/news/alumnus-mark-conway-remembers-important-lessons-learned-spp|title=Alumnus Mark Conway Remembers Important Lessons Learned at SPP|date=October 21, 2014|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[University of Maryland School of Public Policy]]}}</ref> Starting in April 2017, he served as executive director of nonprofit the Baltimore Tree Trust until being named executive vice president of programs and external affairs at the [[Chesapeake Conservancy]] in July 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://baltimorefishbowl.com/stories/nonprofit-baltimore-tree-trust-plant-75-trees-outside-camden-yards/|title=Nonprofit Baltimore Tree Trust to Plant 75 Trees Outside Camden Yards|date=April 13, 2017|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=Baltimore Fishbowl|first=Ethan|last=McLeod}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.capitalgazette.com/2020/07/17/chesapeake-conservancy-names-mark-conway-candidate-for-baltimore-city-council-executive-vp-of-programs/|title=Chesapeake Conservancy names Mark Conway, candidate for Baltimore City Council, executive VP of programs|date=July 17, 2020|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Capital Gazette]]}}</ref>

==Baltimore City Council==
===Tenure===
[[File:Baltimore City Council Inauguration - Zeke Cohen and Mark Conway (12-5-2024).jpg|thumb|Conway and his family take the [[oath of office]] in December 2024.]]
Conway was first elected to the [[Baltimore City Council]] in 2020, winning a crowded primary election by 200 votes to succeed 'Bill' William B. Henry II, who retired to successfully run for [[Comptroller of Baltimore]]. He was re-elected unopposed in the primary and general election in [[2024 Baltimore City Council election|2024]].

According to September 2024 reporting from ''[[The Baltimore Sun]]'', Conway is one of the council’s least active sponsors of legislation having sponsored or co-sponsored 64 ordinances— 37 of which were enacted, six of which were withdrawn, and 21 that were still in progress at the time— since 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2024/09/26/baltimore-city-council-report-card-mark-conway/|title=Baltimore City Council report card: Mark Conway|first=Brooke|last=Conrad|date=September 26, 2024|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref>

===Policy positions===
====Inspector general====
In May 2026, Conway disagreed with mayor [[Brandon Scott]]'s proposal to have the city's [[inspector general]] Isabel Mercedes Cumming report directly to the city law department under the mayor's office, which Conway said would threaten the independence of the watchdog role.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wbal.com/conway-criticizes-scott-oversight-proposal-as-threat-to-inspector-general|title=Councilman Conway criticizes mayor’s oversight proposal as threat to IG|date=May 18, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|first=Chris|last=Cichon|website=[[WBAL (AM)]]}}</ref> He introduced a ballot question to have voters decide if the inspector general should be a "custodian" with direct access to city records, but Scott's lawyers stated this would break the Maryland Public Information Act.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/local-government/isabel-cumming-brandon-scott-ig-baltimore-records-NHJJFIX2QRFFVHLTTHH4MPHFOU/#selection-2633.27-2633.50|title=Can a referendum protect IG records access? Mayor Scott’s lawyers say no.|first=Emily|last=Opilo|date=May 6, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Banner]]}}</ref>

====Energy and environment====
In October 2021, mayor Brandon Scott signed Conway's legislation directing the city's three public [[pension]] funds to [[Fossil fuel divestment|divest from fossil fuels]] within five years.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://marylandmatters.org/2021/10/05/baltimore-mayor-signs-bill-mandating-city-pension-funds-divest-from-fossil-fuels/|title=Baltimore Mayor Signs Bill Mandating City Pension Funds Divest From Fossil Fuels|first=Josh|last=Kurtz|date=October 5, 2021|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> A month later in November 2021, [[Wes Moore]]— whom Conway had endorsed in the [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election]]— named Conway his "personal climate hero" in an interview.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://marylandmatters.org/2021/11/23/wes-moore-on-the-climate-crisis-make-generational-change-in-order-to-bend-the-curve/|title=Wes Moore on the Climate Crisis: ‘Make Generational Change in Order to Bend the Curve’|first=Josh|last=Kurtz|date=November 23, 2021|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> Conway supports the Healthy Harbor campaign to clean up Baltimore's [[Inner Harbor]] and make it swimmable.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bayjournal.com/columns/stewards_corner/from-paris-to-the-patapsco-urban-swimming-is-having-a-moment/article_97fb14c6-63e2-11ef-bd98-2fe9b80a6a13.html|title=From Paris to the Patapsco: Urban swimming is having a moment|first=Jake|last=Solyst|date=September 13, 2024|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=Bay Journal}}</ref>

After sending a letter to the Scott administration questioning whether the city would renew its conduit system agreement with [[Baltimore Gas and Electric]] following numerous fires within the system, the city ultimately announced it would not renew the agreement in June 2026.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/baltimore-city-councilmember-mark-conway-conduit-system-future-mayor-brandon-scott-bge|title=Baltimore City opts out of renewing conduit system agreement with BGE|first=Jessica|last=Babb|date=June 5, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[WBFF]]}}</ref>

====Public safety====
In July 2022, Conway endorsed Sam Cogen for [[Baltimore City Sheriff's Office (Maryland)|Baltimore City Sheriff]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/ten-city-officials-endorse-sam-cogen-for-sheriff-we-want-to-see-some-new-leadership|title=10 city officials endorse Sam Cogen for sheriff: 'We want to see some new leadership'|date=July 6, 2022|first=Amy|last=Simpson|website=[[WBFF]]}}</ref>

As chair of the council's Public Safety Committee, Conway proposed treatment for drug users and jobs for drug dealers, further demanding the city provide a plan to dismantle open air drug markets in April 2026.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/baltimore-councilman-conway-dismantle-open-air-drug-markets/|title=Baltimore councilman demands plan to dismantle city's open-air drug markets|first=Mike|last=Hellgren|date=April 14, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[CBS News]]}}</ref> He was removed as chair of the committee following restructuring by council president [[Zeke Cohen]] in September 2026, a move which Conway characterized as retaliation for his disagreements with other councilmembers on issues such as on the inspector general's role.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/local-government/mark-conway-zeke-cohen-city-council-N4CUI2KMN5HQROXZ3JX2KNF26Q/|title=Baltimore council shake-up: Conway removed from key public safety post|date=September 10, 2026|access-date=September 11, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Banner]]|first1=Emily|last1=Opilo|first2=Jasmine|last2=Vaughn-Hall}}</ref>

===2026 congressional campaign===
In October 2025, Conway announced his campaign for the [[U.S. House of Representatives]] in the [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Maryland|2026 election]], challenging incumbent Democrat [[Kweisi Mfume]] in [[MD-07]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wbal.com/kweisi-mfume-unfazed-by-primary-challenge-mark-conway-says-hes-never-run-without-an-apponent|title=Mfume unfazed by primary challenge, says he’s “never run without an opponent”|first=Katarina|last=Hein|date=October 30, 2025|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[WBAL (AM)]]}}</ref> Conway criticized Mfume for receiving donations of nearly $35,000 from [[AIPAC]] as well as thousands from [[defense contractors]] such as [[Lockheed Martin]] and [[Northrop Grumman]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/04/26/conway-blasts-mfume-over-campaign-donations-from-interest-groups-sunday-enough-is-enough/|title=Conway blasts Mfume over campaign donations from interest groups: ‘Enough is enough’|first=Matt|last=Hubbard|date=April 26, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref>

In April 2026, Conway challenged Mfume to a debate which Mfume declined, calling Conway a "showhorse" who had lied about Mfume accepting donations from corporate [[political action committee]]s.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/04/27/mark-conway-kweisi-mfume-maryland-primary-debate/|title=Councilman Mark Conway dares Rep. Kweisi Mfume to debate, gets sharp rebuff|date=April 27, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Sun]]|first=Tinashe|last=Chingarande}}</ref> At a May 2026 candidate forum hosted by the local advocacy groups and the [[SEIU]], Conway and Mfume notably clashed over Mfume's advanced age at 77 years old and support for [[American military aid to Israel]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/05/27/mfume-conway-baltimore-city-forum/|title=Mfume, Conway squabble over age at Baltimore City forum|date=May 27, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Sun]]|first=Tinashe|last=Chingarande}}</ref>

In the Democratic primary election on June 23, 2026, Conway lost to Mfume by a wide margin with 24.5% to Mfume's 70.1% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/maryland-congressional-races-primary-mfume-UR4SXTLFY5CWHPC6IMZTWLJSOM/|title=Maryland congressional races: Mfume defeats Conway in Democratic primary|date=June 23, 2026|accessdate=June 26, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Banner]]|first=Emily|last=Opilo}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Conway resides in [[Guilford, Baltimore]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2020/05/12/voter-guide-mark-conway-city-council-4-baltimore-city/|title=Voter guide: Mark Conway, City Council (4), Baltimore City|date=May 12, 2020|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref>

